1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:07,333
Many-to-many means each left row can meet many right rows, and each right row can meet many left rows.

2
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:09,444
A student takes many courses.

3
00:00:09,444 --> 00:00:11,486
A course has many students.

4
00:00:11,486 --> 00:00:14,819
You cannot put course_id on students.

5
00:00:14,819 --> 00:00:16,888
That allows only one course.

6
00:00:16,888 --> 00:00:20,222
You cannot put student_id on courses.

7
00:00:20,222 --> 00:00:22,416
That allows only one student.

8
00:00:22,416 --> 00:00:25,236
You cannot put a list of ids in a cell.

9
00:00:25,236 --> 00:00:26,972
You add a third table.

10
00:00:26,972 --> 00:00:28,277
Call it enrollment.

11
00:00:28,277 --> 00:00:32,138
It holds student_id and course_id.

12
00:00:32,138 --> 00:00:34,861
Together they are usually the primary key.

13
00:00:34,861 --> 00:00:36,875
Each pair is one enrollment.

14
00:00:36,875 --> 00:00:39,458
Maya in Harbor Light is one row.

15
00:00:39,458 --> 00:00:42,083
Maya in Quiet Fields is another.

16
00:00:42,083 --> 00:00:44,708
Jordan in Harbor Light is a third.

17
00:00:44,708 --> 00:00:47,916
The same two foreign keys, two parents.

18
00:00:47,916 --> 00:00:52,138
If the relationship has a grade, the grade lives on enrollment.

19
00:00:52,138 --> 00:00:57,611
It is a fact about the pair, not about the student alone, not about the course alone.

20
00:00:57,611 --> 00:01:02,263
That is how you notice you needed the middle table even if you tried to avoid it.

21
00:01:02,263 --> 00:01:07,333
Do not name the middle table student_course unless your team likes that style.

22
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:12,833
A real noun is clearer: enrollment, assignment, line_item.

23
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:15,805
Unique on the pair prevents double enrollment.

24
00:01:15,805 --> 00:01:19,361
A surrogate enrollment_id is optional.

25
00:01:19,361 --> 00:01:25,000
If other tables must point at an enrollment, a surrogate is easier to copy.

26
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,500
To list Maya's titles, join students to enrollment to books.

27
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:30,652
Two hops.

28
00:01:30,652 --> 00:01:31,791
That is normal.

29
00:01:31,791 --> 00:01:35,263
Hiding the middle table in a view is fine for reading.

30
00:01:35,263 --> 00:01:37,222
Writing still goes through the pair.

31
00:01:37,222 --> 00:01:45,458
On the diagram, two crow's feet into the middle box, or two many-to-many marks that you then explode into a box.

32
00:01:45,458 --> 00:01:46,527
Explode them.

33
00:01:46,527 --> 00:01:49,458
SQL has no native many-to-many column.

34
00:01:49,458 --> 00:01:50,722
It has tables.

35
00:01:50,722 --> 00:01:52,833
The relationship is a table.

36
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:58,472
Once you see it, bookstore carts, tags, and authors-of-books are the same shape.

37
00:01:58,472 --> 00:02:00,763
Three-way relationships exist.

38
00:02:00,763 --> 00:02:03,680
A student, a course, and a term.

39
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:09,041
If grade is for Maya in Harbor Light in spring, the key may be all three.

40
00:02:09,041 --> 00:02:11,291
Do not hide term in a comment.

41
00:02:11,291 --> 00:02:14,597
The middle table can have more than two foreign keys.

42
00:02:14,597 --> 00:02:15,986
It is still a table.

43
00:02:15,986 --> 00:02:21,680
Symmetric relationships, like books that are similar to books, still use a middle table.

44
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:29,083
Order the ids or use a CHECK so you do not store both A-B and B-A unless both are facts.

45
00:02:29,083 --> 00:02:31,986
Tags are many-to-many with a small parent.

46
00:02:31,986 --> 00:02:34,472
Do not comma-list tags on the book.

47
00:02:34,472 --> 00:02:36,583
That was first normal form again.

48
00:02:36,583 --> 00:02:39,305
The middle table is book_tag.

49
00:02:39,305 --> 00:02:43,819
When the pair has no extra attributes, people call it a pure junction.

50
00:02:43,819 --> 00:02:46,041
It is still an entity in SQL.

51
00:02:46,041 --> 00:02:47,111
Give it a name.

52
00:02:47,111 --> 00:02:52,513
Permissions of users to projects are many-to-many with a role attribute on the pair.

53
00:02:52,513 --> 00:02:54,875
The role is why the pair is not pure.

54
00:02:54,875 --> 00:02:56,555
That attribute will grow.

55
00:02:56,555 --> 00:02:59,208
Plan a real noun: membership.

56
00:02:59,208 --> 00:03:05,930
Query patterns: all courses for a student, all students for a course, all pairs with grade A.

57
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:10,152
Each pattern is two joins or one, starting from the middle.

58
00:03:10,152 --> 00:03:13,625
Starting from the middle is often the clearest SQL.

59
00:03:13,625 --> 00:03:15,166
FROM enrollment JOIN ...

60
00:03:15,166 --> 00:03:20,444
The relationship table is the FROM table when the question is about the relationship.

61
00:03:20,444 --> 00:03:23,402
That sentence saves a lot of nested guesses.

62
00:03:23,402 --> 00:03:25,000
Draw three boxes.

63
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,097
Put the facts on the correct one.

64
00:03:27,097 --> 00:03:31,416
If the fact needs both parents, you already know where it sits.

65
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:35,541
If you are about to add a second foreign key to a parent, pause.

66
00:03:35,541 --> 00:03:38,430
Two parents usually want a child of their own.

67
00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:40,041
That child is the pair.

68
00:03:40,041 --> 00:03:41,416
Give the pair a noun.

69
00:03:41,416 --> 00:03:42,791
Give the pair a key.

70
00:03:42,791 --> 00:03:45,208
Give the pair any extra facts.

71
00:03:45,208 --> 00:03:48,500
Join from the pair when the question is about the pair.

72
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:50,638
That habit keeps SQL short.

73
00:03:50,638 --> 00:03:54,611
Short SQL is how you know the model matches the sentence.

74
00:03:54,611 --> 00:03:58,708
Long SQL with two lists in a cell is how you know it does not.

75
00:03:58,708 --> 00:04:01,263
Draw three boxes until it is boring.

76
00:04:01,263 --> 00:04:04,444
Boring three boxes beat a clever array.

77
00:04:04,444 --> 00:04:06,555
The relationship is a table.

78
00:04:06,555 --> 00:04:09,888
Say it until the clever array looks embarrassing.

79
00:04:09,888 --> 00:04:18,527
Beginners connect two tables with two foreign keys pointing at each other and then wonder why a query cannot list a student's courses.

80
00:04:18,527 --> 00:04:20,083
You need a third table.

81
00:04:20,083 --> 00:04:28,972
Many-to-many means each instance on this side can associate with many on that side, and each on that side with many on this side.

82
00:04:28,972 --> 00:04:30,638
Students and courses.

83
00:04:30,638 --> 00:04:36,861
Products and orders, if an order contains many products and a product appears on many orders.

84
00:04:36,861 --> 00:04:40,652
Authors and books, when a book can have several authors.

85
00:04:40,754 --> 00:04:43,620
A direct foreign key cannot hold a list.

86
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:48,509
A comma-separated course list in a student row is not a relationship.

87
00:04:48,509 --> 00:04:50,565
It is a string you will regret.

88
00:04:50,565 --> 00:04:56,912
The junction table, also called an associate or bridge table, holds one row per pairing.

89
00:04:56,912 --> 00:05:03,759
student_id and course_id together are often the primary key of enrollments.

90
00:05:03,759 --> 00:05:07,370
Each of those columns is a foreign key to its parent.

91
00:05:07,370 --> 00:05:12,870
A student enrolled in three courses produces three rows, not three columns.

92
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:17,690
A course with thirty students produces thirty rows, not a nested table.

93
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:25,537
Attributes of the pairing belong on the junction: enrolled_on, grade, quantity on an order line.

94
00:05:25,537 --> 00:05:29,995
Quantity is not an attribute of product alone or order alone.

95
00:05:29,995 --> 00:05:32,842
It is an attribute of this product on this order.

96
00:05:32,842 --> 00:05:36,579
That is how you know the third table is real, not a hack.

97
00:05:36,579 --> 00:05:40,870
If the pairing has no attributes, the two keys are still enough.

98
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:43,412
Do not skip the table because it looks empty.

99
00:05:43,412 --> 00:05:44,981
The pair is the fact.

100
00:05:44,981 --> 00:05:49,106
Cardinality to the junction is one-to-many from each parent.

101
00:05:49,106 --> 00:05:51,967
Student to enrollments is one-to-many.

102
00:05:51,967 --> 00:05:54,551
Course to enrollments is one-to-many.

103
00:05:54,551 --> 00:05:59,231
The many-to-many is the view from the two ends, looking through the bridge.

104
00:05:59,231 --> 00:06:03,579
Query with two joins: students to enrollments to courses.

105
00:06:03,579 --> 00:06:07,245
Filter on the junction when the question is about the pairing.

106
00:06:07,245 --> 00:06:15,870
Unique on (student_id, course_id) prevents double enrollment unless repeats are a business event.

107
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:24,398
If repeats are allowed, add a surrogate and a uniqueness rule that matches the real world, such as one open enrollment at a time.

108
00:06:24,398 --> 00:06:32,940
Draw the diamond or the crow's feet through the junction, not as a mysterious line with many on both ends and no table.

109
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:38,384
A line with many and many and no box in the middle is an unfinished sentence.

110
00:06:38,384 --> 00:06:41,065
Finish it with a table name you can CREATE.

111
00:06:41,065 --> 00:06:45,079
Then the SQL works because the grain is one pairing per row.

